  "name": "Indigenismo (Peruvian Andean-nationalist art music)",
  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "Peruvian indigenist art music is the early-twentieth-century concert school that built piano pieces, art songs, zarzuelas and orchestral works on Andean pentatonic melody and huayno and yaraví rhythms. Daniel Alomía Robles, whose zarzuela El cóndor pasa dates from 1913, and Teodoro Valcárcel of Puno led it, and the immigrant composers André Sas and Rodolfo Holzmann extended it in Lima from the 1920s and 1930s.",
